.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt LUMTAIL-AGENT 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lumtail-agent
.Nd bounded JSON log queries for scripts and agents
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Read this page to query logs with a fixed output budget and recover full
records from the returned source locations.
.Xr lumtail 1
provides the command reference and filter grammar.
.Pp
Start with a small snapshot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --limit 20 application.log
lumtail --agent --limit 20 --min-level warn application.log
lumtail --agent --limit 20 --where service=api application.log
.Ed
.Pp
Each command writes one compact JSON object per matching logical record to
standard output.
Narrow the selection before raising its record or string limits.
Diagnostics and optional statistics use standard error.
.Pp
The
.Fl -agent
and
.Fl -output Cm agent
forms select the same output.
Defaults are 100 matching records, 2048 encoded bytes per variable string and
20 decoded message lines.
.Sh RECORD FORMAT
Every agent record contains these six keys:
.Bl -tag -width timestamp
.It Cm timestamp
Producer timestamp string, or JSON null when absent.
Timestamp text is kept as supplied by the parser.
.It Cm level
One of UNKNOWN, TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR or CRITICAL.
.It Cm message
Normalized message string, including attached multiline continuations.
.It Cm file
Input path as supplied in the invocation, or
.Ql -
for standard input.
.It Cm line
Integer identifying the first physical source line, starting at 1.
.It Cm format
Selected parser name, such as
.Cm json , syslog
or
.Cm plain .
.El
.Pp
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"timestamp":null,"level":"ERROR","message":"Connection failed","file":"app.log","line":42,"format":"json"}
.Ed
.Pp
Records keep input order, even when timestamps are out of sequence.
JSON string escaping replaces invalid UTF-8 with U+FFFD and escapes control
characters, Unicode line separators and direction controls.
Agent output contains no ANSI coloring.
.Pp
Query the machine-readable format without opening input or loading rules:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent-schema
.Ed
.Pp
Its schema identifier is
.Ql urn:lumtail:agent:v1 .
Read keys by name; the standard keys remain present when optional context and
clipping markers are added.
.Sh FIELD NAMES
.Fl -list-fields
examines the first 1000 selected records, or
.Fl -limit
records, and prints one line per format after input ends:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"format":"json","records":1,"fields":{"level":1,"message":1,"user":1}}
.Ed
.Pp
Each count is the number of records that set the name.
Use the names with
.Fl -where , -filter , -agent-fields
and
.Fl -template ;
they differ by parser.
Names are clipped at 128 encoded bytes and marked with
.Ql "truncated":true ;
past 256 names a format adds
.Ql "more_fields":true .
Set
.Fl -duration-ms
with
.Fl -follow
or an open pipe.
.Sh COMPACT OUTPUT
.Fl -output Cm agent-compact
selects the same records, limits and clipping as agent output and writes them
without repeated key names or paths:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"schema":"urn:lumtail:agent-compact:v1","columns":["timestamp","level","message","line","format"]}
{"file":"app.log"}
[null,"ERROR","Connection failed",42,"json"]
.Ed
.Pp
The header object comes before the first record and names the columns of
every record array.
A
.Ql {"file":...}
object comes before each record whose source differs from the previous one;
source names are never clipped.
Selected fields become extra columns in their requested order.
A clipped record ends with one
.Ql {"truncated":[...]}
object that uses the names of
.Cm truncated_fields .
Zero selected records produce empty output.
.Ql lumtail --output agent-compact --agent-schema
prints the line schema
.Ql urn:lumtail:agent-compact:v1 .
.Sh SELECTED CONTEXT
Use
.Fl -agent-fields
to add normalized context fields:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --limit 20 --agent-fields service,user,src_ip app.log
.Ed
.Pp
The optional
.Cm fields
object contains requested keys in selection order.
Values are strings when present and JSON null when missing.
A present empty string remains distinct from a missing field.
A record with a service and user but no address adds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"fields":{"service":"api","user":"alice","src_ip":null}
.Ed
.Pp
Names select literal keys in the normalized event field map, including
namespaced keys such as
.Cm syslog_sd.auth@32473.user .
For the record location, use the standard
.Cm file
and
.Cm line
keys; context selection addresses the field map.
.Pp
Select up to 16 unique names.
Each name contains 1 through 128 ASCII letters, digits,
.Ql _ ,
.Ql . ,
.Ql - ,
.Ql @
or
.Ql % .
Spaces and tabs surrounding comma-separated names are ignored.
The complete option value is limited to 4096 bytes.
A repeated option replaces the earlier list; invalid selections return status
2 before input is opened.
The option requires agent output.
.Sh BYTE AND LINE BUDGETS
.Fl -max-message-bytes Ar bytes
limits encoded JSON string content, excluding surrounding quotes.
Escapes count toward this budget: a six-byte Unicode escape consumes six bytes.
The default is 2048.
.Pp
The message, timestamp and filename each have this byte budget.
Timestamp and filename also have a one-line limit; the parser name is capped
at 64 encoded bytes.
The message obeys
.Fl -max-lines-per-record Ar count ,
whose default is 20.
Decoded LF, NEL and Unicode line/paragraph separators count as line boundaries.
.Pp
All selected context string values share one additional
.Fl -max-message-bytes
budget, spent in selection order.
Each selected value also obeys the decoded line limit.
An empty string or null consumes zero string-content bytes; unused bytes from
an earlier clipped value remain available to later values.
Keys, JSON punctuation and null values have separate fixed overhead bounded
by the selection's name/count limits.
.Pp
Both budget options accept positive decimal integers and require agent output.
They affect output strings after parsing and filtering.
For a smaller query:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --limit 20 --multiline indent \e
  --max-message-bytes 1024 --max-lines-per-record 10 app.log
.Ed
.Sh CLIPPING
Clipping keeps a prefix ending at a complete UTF-8 scalar or JSON escape.
A clipped record adds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"truncated":true,"truncated_fields":["message"]
.Ed
.Pp
The array names each affected standard field.
Selected values use
.Ql fields\&.
followed by the exact literal key, for example
.Ql fields.syslog_sd.auth@32473.user .
Complete records omit both clipping keys.
.Pp
Add
.Fl -fail-on-truncation
to return status 1 before writing any clipped record.
This includes clipped timestamps, filenames, parser names and selected context.
The diagnostic gives the source filename and first physical line.
Complete records written before it stay on standard output.
.Pp
For full content at the reported location:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --output json --where line=42 --limit 1 app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Use the same rules, module and multiline options as the initial query.
The full JSON record contains all normalized fields and
.Cm raw ;
its schema is available through
.Fl -record-schema .
.Sh FILTERING
Selection happens before clipping and before the matching-record limit.
Combine a severity threshold, exact fields and compound expressions as needed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --limit 20 --min-level error \e
  --where service=api --agent-fields service,duration_ms,status \e
  --filter 'duration_ms>=1000 AND status>=500' app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Every supplied selection must match.
The command above requires ERROR or CRITICAL, exact
.Ql service=api ,
and both numeric thresholds.
Numeric comparisons use normalized field strings as exact decimal values.
.Pp
For alternatives and exclusions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --limit 20 \e
  --filter 'level=error AND (user=admin OR src_ip~192.168.*)' app.log
lumtail --agent --limit 20 --filter 'NOT service=healthcheck' app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Missing fields fail comparisons; NOT negates that result.
Consequently the second command also selects records with a missing service.
The full grammar and severity aliases are in
.Xr lumtail 1 .
.Sh LIVE INPUT AND COMPLETION
Set a duration when following a file or reading a producer that stays open:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --follow --duration-ms 5000 --limit 50 \e
  --min-level error app.log
command | lumtail --agent --duration-ms 5000 --limit 20
.Ed
.Pp
The duration accepts 1 through 86400000 milliseconds and starts after worker
initialization.
Collection stops at the deadline, with the reader's polling granularity.
An unfinished physical line is discarded; complete assembled and queued
records finish processing.
The reader checks deadlines between physical lines, including buffered lines.
.Pp
The limit applies globally across files, in input order, after every filter.
Reaching it cancels input waiting and workers while preserving the selected
ordered output.
Ordinary downstream pipe closure also cancels waiting work.
Processing failures discovered in queued work still return status 1.
.Sh PIPELINE ASSERTIONS
.Fl -expect-none
requires zero selected records;
.Fl -expect-some
requires at least one.
These mutually exclusive options keep the normal record output.
Redirect it when only the exit status matters:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --expect-none --errors-only app.log > /dev/null
lumtail --agent --expect-some --where event_type=alert app.log \e
  > /dev/null
.Ed
.Pp
An unmet assertion returns 3; input, output, sandbox and processing failures
return 1 and take precedence.
The complete status table is in
.Xr lumtail 1 .
Use
.Ql set -o pipefail
in a supporting shell when a producer's failure must propagate through its
pipeline.
.Sh INTERFACE VERSION
Omitting
.Fl -api-version
selects the permanent CLI API 1.
An explicit
.Fl -api-version Ar 1
selects the same behavior independently of the executable release version.
The command
.Ql lumtail --cli-contract
prints the published definition.
Installed definitions and extension consumers are under
.Pa share/lumtail/contracts/cli/
in the installation prefix.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr lumtail 1 ,
.Xr lumtail-rules 5 ,
.Xr lumtail-formats 7
.Sh LIMITS
Byte budgets measure encoded JSON content; model token counts need their own
budget.
Recovering a clipped filename requires the invocation's input list or another
query with a larger cap.
.Pp
A matching-record limit can wait indefinitely when no record matches live input.
Limits and assertions describe the observed complete records; unread input can
contain further matches.
There is no aggregate truncation marker when the record limit is reached.
Physical-line overflow is an input error, separate from agent clipping.
.Pp
The duration bounds collection rather than total process runtime.
Queued output and blocking filesystem/output operations add to the run time.
For safety rules concerning untrusted log content, see
.Pa share/doc/lumtail/docs/safety.md .
